C++ Function Overloading
Description

This C++ program demonstrates function overloading using multiple add() functions.

The add() functions have the same name but different parameter lists. The compiler selects the appropriate function based on the number and type of arguments passed.

Classes Used

No class is used in this program.

The program demonstrates function overloading using three functions.

1. add(int, int) Function

The add() function accepts two integer values.

It takes two int parameters.
It returns their sum.
It is called when two integer values are passed.
2. add(double, double) Function

The second add() function accepts two decimal values.

It takes two double parameters.
It returns their sum.
It is called when two decimal values are passed.
3. add(int, int, int) Function

The third add() function accepts three integer values.

It takes three int parameters.
It returns their sum.
It is called when three integer values are passed.
Main Function

In the main() function, the overloaded add() functions are called with different arguments:

10, 20 → adds two integers.
2.5, 3.7 → adds two double values.
10, 20, 30 → adds three integers.

The results are then displayed on the screen.

Output
Sum of two integers: 30
Sum of two doubles: 6.2
Sum of three integers: 60
OOP Concept Used

Compile-Time Polymorphism — Function Overloading

              add()
          /     |      \
         /      |       \
 add(int,int)   |   add(int,int,int)
                |
        add(double,double)

The same function name add() performs different operations depending on the parameters passed. This is called function overloading.